+# GitHub traffic collector
+
+`github_traffic.py` records how much the Bazel rulesets are used, as seen by
+GitHub: page views, git clones, top referring sites and the most visited
+content. It reads the documented
+[traffic API](https://docs.github.com/en/rest/metrics/traffic) and keeps the
+numbers in CSV files that anyone can ingest.
+
+Two GitHub limits shape the design:
+
+- Traffic is only visible to accounts with **push access** to a repository.
+  An outside party cannot collect it, so the script runs with a token from
+  someone who has that access, and publishes the result where the SIG can read
+  it without a privileged token.
+- GitHub only keeps the **last 14 days**. Every run merges the current window
+  into the stored history, so running at least every 13 days (daily is
+  recommended) gives a complete, gap-free time series.
+
+The script needs only Python 3.8+ and its standard library.
+
+## Token
+
+The token is read from the environment, never from the command line. The
+script looks at `$GITHUB_TRAFFIC_TOKEN`, then `$GITHUB_TOKEN`, then
+`$GH_TOKEN` (or the variable named with `--token-env`).
+
+Create a
+[fine-grained personal access token](https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens/new)
+owned by an account with push access to the repositories:
+
+- **Resource owner**: the organization, e.g. `bazelbuild` (the org may need to
+  approve the token).
+- **Repository access**: all repositories, or the ones to collect.
+- **Repository permissions**: **Administration: Read-only**. This is the
+  permission GitHub requires for the traffic endpoints. Metadata: Read-only is
+  added automatically.
+
+To publish to a Gist (see below) the token that writes the Gist needs the
+account permission **Gists: Read and write**. Gists belong to a user, not an
+org, so this is usually a second fine-grained token with the user as resource
+owner. Put it in `$GIST_TOKEN` (or name its variable with `--gist-token-env`);
+without it the traffic token is used for the Gist too.
+
+Repositories the token cannot read are reported on stderr and skipped; the
+data of the others is still saved, and the exit code is 1.
+
+## Running it
+
+```sh
+export GITHUB_TRAFFIC_TOKEN=github_pat_...
+
+# Every public, non-archived, non-fork repository of the org that the token
+# can push to.
+python3 tools/github_traffic/github_traffic.py --org bazelbuild
+
+# Only the rulesets.
+python3 tools/github_traffic/github_traffic.py --org bazelbuild --match '^rules_'
+
+# An explicit list.
+python3 tools/github_traffic/github_traffic.py \
+  --repo bazelbuild/rules_python --repo bazelbuild/rules_apple
+
+# A list kept in a file: one OWNER/NAME per line, # starts a comment.
+python3 tools/github_traffic/github_traffic.py --repos-file repos.txt
+```
+
+Each run prints the 14-day totals per repository:
+
+```
+repository                  views  visitors    clones   cloners
+bazelbuild/rules_apple       4210       900        77        30
+bazelbuild/rules_python      1520       610       340       120
+```
+
+and updates the CSV files in `--output-dir` (default `traffic-data/`).
+Run `github_traffic.py --help` for all options.
+
+| Exit code | Meaning                                                       |
+| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| 0         | Every repository was collected and the data was written.      |
+| 1         | Some or all repositories failed, or the data was not written. |
+| 2         | Configuration error: no token, no repositories, bad Gist ID.  |
+
+## Output
+
+All files are CSV with a header row, sorted for stable diffs. Dates are UTC.
+
+| File            | One row per                        | Columns                                                    |
+| --------------- | ---------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `views.csv`     | repository and day                 | `repo,date,count,uniques`                                  |
+| `clones.csv`    | repository and day                 | `repo,date,count,uniques`                                  |
+| `summary.csv`   | collection date and repository     | `collected,repo,views,views_uniques,clones,clones_uniques` |
+| `referrers.csv` | collection date, repo and referrer | `collected,repo,referrer,count,uniques`                    |
+| `paths.csv`     | collection date, repo and path     | `collected,repo,path,title,count,uniques`                  |
+
+- `views.csv` and `clones.csv` are the daily time series. A day stays in
+  GitHub's window for 14 days, so it is seen by several runs; the largest
+  numbers seen are kept, which means the current (partial) day is completed by
+  later runs and re-running never lowers a recorded value. Days without any
+  traffic may be absent; treat them as zero.
+- `summary.csv`, `referrers.csv` and `paths.csv` are dated snapshots of
+  GitHub's 14-day totals. Unique visitors cannot be added up across days, so
+  `summary.csv` is the place to compare repositories by unique visitors.
+  GitHub reports only the top 10 referrers and paths. A second run on the same
+  date replaces that date's snapshot.
+
+## Publishing to a Gist
+
+Create a Gist once (it can be secret), with any placeholder file, and pass its
+ID:
+
+```sh
+python3 tools/github_traffic/github_traffic.py --org bazelbuild --gist-id 0123456789abcdef
+```
+
+The script first reads the CSV files already in the Gist (and in
+`--output-dir`), merges the new data into them and writes them back, so the
+Gist always holds the full history and the script can run from any machine
+without keeping local state. Pass `--output-dir ''` to skip the local copy.
+Unchanged files are not re-uploaded.
+
+Consumers read the latest data without a token, for example:
+
+```
+https://gist.githubusercontent.com/<owner>/<gist-id>/raw/views.csv
+```
+
+## Running on a schedule
+
+### GitHub Actions
+
+[`.github/workflows/github_traffic.yaml`](../../.github/workflows/github_traffic.yaml)
+runs the collector daily (and on demand from the Actions tab). It does nothing
+until it is configured in this repository's settings:
+
+| Setting              | Kind     | Value                                                                                     |
+| -------------------- | -------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `TRAFFIC_TOKEN`      | secret   | The traffic token described above.                                                        |
+| `TRAFFIC_GIST_TOKEN` | secret   | Optional: the Gist token, if it is a separate token.                                      |
+| `TRAFFIC_GIST_ID`    | variable | The Gist to publish to. Without it, data is only kept as a workflow artifact for 90 days. |
+| `TRAFFIC_ORG`        | variable | Optional: the org to collect, default `bazelbuild`.                                       |
+| `TRAFFIC_MATCH`      | variable | Optional: a regex on repository names, e.g. `^rules_`.                                    |
+
+Each run also attaches the CSV files as an artifact and shows the 14-day
+totals in the run summary.
+
+### cron
+
+Someone with push access can run it from any machine instead, e.g. daily at
+06:17 UTC:
+
+```
+17 6 * * * cd /path/to/SIG-rules-authors && GITHUB_TRAFFIC_TOKEN=... python3 tools/github_traffic/github_traffic.py --org bazelbuild --gist-id 0123456789abcdef
+```
+
+## Tests
+
+The tests use a fake HTTP transport and need neither network nor token:
+
+```sh
+python3 tools/github_traffic/github_traffic_test.py
+```
